// Data type specifies the type of data a variable can store.
// Basically specifies memory size for the variable.
//
// # There are 4 Data types in C
// 1. Basic
// 2. Derived
// 3. Enumeration
// 4. Void
//
// # Basic Data Type:
// 1. int     %i,%d     2 or 4 bytes     stores whole numbers
//                 Range: -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647
//                     or (0 to 4,294,967,296)
//
// 2. float     %f        4 bytes        stores fractional numbers  6 digit accuracy
//                   Range: -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647
//                     or (0 to 4,294,967,296) 
//
// 3. double    %lf       8 bytes        stores fractional numbers 15 digit accuracy
//                  Range: -9.223372037×10¹⁸ to 9.223372036×10¹⁸
//                     or (0 to 1.844674407×10¹⁹)
//
// 4. char      %c        1 byte         stores a single character or ASCII 
//                 Range : (-128) to (127)
//                  or (0 to 255)
//
// # Derived Data type:
// 1. array
// 2. pointer
// 3. structure
// 4. union
//
// # Enumeration Data Type:
// 1. enum
// 2. typedef
//
// #Void Data Type:
//  void
//  It's used to specify that no value is present.
//  it can be used as function return type, argument and points to void [void *malloc(size_t size);]

